# ns3 map — working rules

## Use the ns3-kg MCP tools instead of reading files

This project has an `ns3-kg` MCP server indexing all of `ns-3.48/`. For any
question about the ns-3 code — where something is, its signature, who calls
it, what attributes/traces a class has — call the MCP tools first; only read
a file directly after a tool has given you its path and line range (then use
`get_source`, or the Read tool for >200-line stretches).

- Discover names: `search_symbols` → exact decl: `get_signature` → body:
  `get_source` → callers: `find_usages` (textual candidates — verify).
- ns-3 specifics: `get_typeid_attributes` (config knobs + traces per class),
  `find_trace_sources`, `get_inheritance_chain`,
  `resolve_config_path` (Config strings → owning class).
- If answers look wrong, call `get_index_status`; if it warns, ask me to run
  `ns3kg-index` (do not run it yourself against a dirty tree).

## ns-3 wifi map (where to aim lookups, all under `src/wifi/model/`)

- `ap-wifi-mac`, `sta-wifi-mac` (+ base `wifi-mac`): AP/STA MAC logic —
  association, beacons, per-AC queues.
- `txop`, `qos-txop`, `channel-access-manager`: EDCA channel access,
  TXOP handling, block ack agreements.
- `frame-exchange-manager` (+ `qos-`, `he/he-` variants): the TX/RX state
  machine per amendment; most "why was this frame sent" answers live here.
- `he/multi-user-scheduler`, `he/rr-multi-user-scheduler`: DL/UL OFDMA MU
  scheduling decisions (my main work area).
- `wifi-remote-station-manager` + `rate-control/` (Minstrel-HT etc.): rate
  control; per-station state.
- `wifi-phy`, `spectrum-wifi-phy`, `wifi-mac-queue`: PHY and queueing.

## My conventions

- Before changing ANY function signature: `find_usages` on it and check the
  call sites; ns-3 uses callbacks heavily, so textual hits need verifying.
- New simulation code: prefer extending an example in `examples/wireless/`
  over creating scratch files.
- Follow `ns-3.48/CLAUDE.md` (build/test/style) when editing inside the tree.
- Keep explanations of architecture simple and jargon-free.
